INISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KIREN RIJIJU) \n \n(a) to (c): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n****** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n-2- \n \nSTATEMENT REFFERED TO IN REPLY TO THE LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO.*173 FOR 14.3.2017. \n \n \n(a) to (c): All the Central Armed Police Forces (CAPFs) have in place \nappropriate channels of communication  between men and officers in \nrespective organisations. Apart from direct forms of communication/ \ninteraction such as Open Darbar, Sainik Sammelan and personal h earing \netc, which are part of established fora of communication/interaction at \nvarious levels, Information and Communications Technology (ICT) based \ncommunication such as web -based/app-based grievance redressal, mobile \nservices, e-mail etc are also put in use with due security audit as per rules.   \n \n******* "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|11|STARRED   |175", "t": "Wildlife Protection", "head": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n  \nLOK SABHA  \nSTARRED QUESTION Q. No. *175 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 14.03.2017 \n  \n  Wildlife Protection \n \n \n*175  SHRI P. R. SUNDARAM: \n                                   \n Will the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n  \n(a)   whether the Government has taken scientific and innovative measures to control Wild \nAnimal-Human conflict in the country and if so, the details thereof; \n(b) whether the Government has provided adequate funds and infrastructure facilities to \ndevelop the wild life and Bird sanctuaries, Zoological parks in the country to protect \nendangered animals and birds; \n(c) if so, the details of funds allocated and released for the purpose during each of  the last \nthree years and the current year, sanctuary/zoological parks and State- wise; and  \n(d)   the steps taken by the Government to develop Marine parks, Zoological parks and bird \nsanctuaries in the country including Tamil Nadu?  \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF S TATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR ENVIRONMENT, FOREST \nAND CLIMATE CHANGE  \n(SHRI ANIL MADHAV DAVE) \n \n \n(a), (b), (c) and (d)  \n \nA statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n*** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS ( a), (b), (c) AND ( d) OF LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. *175 REGARDING “WILDLIFE \nPROTECTION” RAISED BY SHRI P. R. SUNDARAM  DUE FOR REPLY ON \n14.03.2017. \n \n(a) \n \nThe management of wildlife in the country is taken up in a scientific manner \nby the respective State/Union Territory Govern ments. The Ministry of \nEnvironment, Forest and Climate Change has issued advisories to the \nState/Union Territory Governments for management of human wildlife \nconflict situations. These advisories also indicate the following  important \nactions broadly aiming adoption of scientific principles: \ni. Use of local data in special patterns at Division/Range level with GIS \nbased system.  \nii. Radio telemetry for identification and pattern of  movement of large \nwildlife in case of threat to humans. \niii. Maintaining foraging ground s within forests along boundaries and \naugmenting palatable grasses.  \niv. Identifying and upkeep of wildlife movement corridors, especially \nmigratory patterns. \nv. Payment of ecosystem services principles as an option indentified  \nareas, for encou raging land owners for adopting desirable land use \npractices. In community interest. \n \n(b) and (c) Ministry provides financial assistance to State /Union Territory  \nGovernments  under  various Centrally Sponsored   Scheme viz., Integrated \nDevelopment of Wildlife Habitats (ID WH), ‘Project Tiger ’ and ‘Project \nElephant’ for the scientific management of  wildlife habitats including \ndevelopment of infrastructure  facilities  for wildlife  conservation.   Funds \nare also released for management of Zoological Parks in  the country. Sta te-\nwise details of fund released under these schemes during the last three years \nand the current year is given in the Annexure.   \n(d) Development of Protected Areas including Marine Parks, Zoological Parks \nare the responsibility of the respective state s/operators. However, in case of \nZoological Parks, The Central Zoo Authority provides recognitions as pe r \nprovisions of Wild Life (Protection) Act, 1972. Financial and technical \nassistance is provided by the Ministry/Central Zoo Authority for \nmanagement of Protected Areas and Zoological Parks.  \n \n \n     *** \n \n \n\n \n \nAnnexure \n \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) OF LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. * 175 \nREGARDING “WILDLIFE PROTECTION” RAISED BY SHRI P. R. SUNDARAM DUE FOR REPLY ON \n14.03.2017. \nDetails of funds released to State/ UT Governments under CSS-‘Integrated Development of Wildlife Habitats’ during \nlast three years and the current year \nRs. in lakhs) \nS.No. Name of  States/UTs 2013-14 2014-15 2015-16 2016-17 \n(As on 02.02.2017) \n1 A& N Islands 150.00 00 100.00 118.49 \n2 Andhra Pradesh 00 63.31 0 0 \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 220.439 00 304.02 256.8107 \n4 Assam 138.88 149.11 87.10 0 \n5 Bihar 34.8715 85.249 108.011 100.576 \n6 Chandigarh 0 0 0 26.06514 \n7 Chhattisgarh 408.74 482.087 213.409 278.9453 \n8 Goa 00 00 00 0 \n9 Gujarat 537.84457 634.94 395.798 497.604 \n10 Haryana 00 14.71 99.33 124.6572 \n11 Himachal Pradesh 475.849 430.345 431.837 280.31 \n12 Jammu & Kashmir 485.747 506.761 354.00 336.50626 \n13 Jharkhand 97.7655 101.12 18.62 0 \n14 Karnataka 351.00 483.7769 262.13 325.52 \n15 Kerala 505.782 818.491 967.386 700.317 \n16 Madhya Pradesh 454.354 371.354 394.565 322.265 \n17 Maharashtra 470.772 402.723 277.94 497.35 \n18 Manipur 80.80 129.192 248.919 340.032 \n19 Meghalaya 25.56 44.87 38.3902 55.23 \n20 Mizoram 210.334 131.54134 94.55 1234.95 \n21 Nagaland 15.375 85.155 235.48 357.846 \n22 Odisha 341.7448 350.3229 246.8365 279.65 \n23 Rajasthan 430.884 367.296 314.788 429.99763 \n24 Sikkim 129.27836 169.15643 290.32635 145.52 \n25 Tamil Nadu 277.7918 280.626 113.261 0 \n2"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|11|STARRED   |176", "t": "Allocation of Foodgrains to Kerala", "head": "ORIGINAL IN HINDI \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \nDEPARTMENT OF FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 176 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 14TH MARCH, 2017 \n \nAllocation of Foodgrains to Kerala \n \n*176.  SHRIMATI P.K. SREEMATHI TEACHER: \nSHRI N.K. PREMACHANDRAN: \n \n \nWill the Minister of CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC \nDISTRIBUTION miHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k ea=h   be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government of Kerala  has demanded allocation of more \nfoodgrains for public distribution and if so, the action taken by the \nGovernment thereon; \n \n(b)  the details of demand and allocation of foodgrains to Kerala during \neach of the last two years; \n \n(c)  whether the Government is aware  that the distribution and supply of  \nfoodgrains implemented on the basis of  National Food Security Act have \nadversely affected the Public Distribution System in  Kerala and if so, the \naction taken to protect the existing Public Distribution System in Kerala; and \n \n(d)  whether the Government has  received representations from Kerala  \nseeking extension of time for  implementation of provisions of the National \nFood Security Act so as to protect  the existing Public Distribution System \nand if so, the details of the initiati ves taken by  the Government with the \nsupport of Kerala for continuing the existing ration distribution system in the \nState? \n \n \nA N S W E R \nMINISTER OF CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n(SHRI RAMVILAS PASWAN) \n \n(a) to (d): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n****  \n\\ \n \n \n \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (d) OF THE STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 176 DUE FOR ANSWER ON 14.3.2017 IN THE LOK SABHA \n \n(a) to (d) The National Food Security Act, 2013 (NFSA), which came into force \nwith effect from 5th July, 2013 seeks to provide for food and nutritional security.  \nThe Act provides for coverage of upto 75% of the rural population and upto 50% \nof the urban population for receiving highly subsidized foodgrains under Targeted \nPublic Distribution System ( TPDS), thus covering about two-thirds of the \npopulation at Census 2011 population.  The quota of foodgrains under NFSA for \nthe States/UTs has been fixed considering the coverage of population of the State \nunder the Act and the average annual offtake of foodgrains provided under AAY, \nBPL, APL and Additional APL categories under erstwhile TPDS during the three \nfinancial years prior to enactment of NFSA.     \n \nNFSA has been implemented by Kerala with effect from 1st Nov, 2016 \nand as per the provision of the Act, 118754.083 tons of foodgrains per month is \nbeing allocated to Kerala.  State of Kerala can annually be granted upto a \nmaximum of 14.25 lakh MT of foodgrains while protecting the offtake of foodgrain \nof three years prior to the period when NFSA came into effect.  Foodgrain \nallocation to Kerala is done in terms of the policy which is uniformly applicable to \nall the 36 States/Union Territories.  Kerala has been demanding additional \nallocation of around 2 lakh MT of foodgrain over and above its entitlement under \nNFSA.   \n \nThe allocation of foodgrain made to Kerala during the past two years i.e. \n2014-15 and 2015-16 under the erstwhile TPDS system is tabulated below: \n(Figures in tons) \nAllocation \nYear \nTPDS Normal Additional \nAllocation \nTotal \nAllocation \nAAY BPL APL Total APL BPL \n2014-15 250260 402348 573996 1226 724 247625 120890 1595239 \n2015-16 250260 402348 573996 1226 724 245964 119172 1591860 \n \nThe commitment of the Government is to provide subsidized foodgrain to \nthe poorest section of the society.  Prior to the implementation of NFSA under \nerstwhile TPDS system, 5.96 lakh AAY families (31.47 lakh persons) were \nallocated about 2.50 lakh MT of foodgrain @ Rs.3/2 per kg for rice/wheat \nrespectively and 9.58 lakh BPL families (50.58 lakh persons) were allocated about \n5.02 lakh MT of foodgrain @ Rs.5.65/4.15 per kg for rice/wheat respectively.  \nSubsequent to implementation of NFSA with effect from 1\nst November, 2016, 5.96 \nlakh AAY families (25.59 lakh persons) and 129 lakh persons under priority \nhousehold category are being annually allocated 10.25 lakh MT of foodgrain @ \nRs.3/2 per kg for rice/wheat respectively.   Thus under NFSA, a larger section of \nthe poorer people are getting foodgrain at more subsidized prices than before.  \n \nKerala has been demanding additional allocation of around 2 lakh MT of \nfoodgrain over and above its annual entitlement of 14.25 lakh MT under NFSA.  \nKerala has been informed that the additional allocation of foodgrain can be made \nat Economic Cost as  per the extant policy of Government of India.  Moreover, the \nState Government can also explore procurement of foodgrain under the Open \nMarket Sales Scheme (OMSS) as the cost of foodgrain under OMSS is \ncomparatively lesser than the Economic Cost.   \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|11|STARRED   |177", "t": "e-NAM", "head": "��������������������\n����������������������������������������\n�\n����������\n��������������������������\n�����������������������������\n�\n�����������������������������\n�������������������������������\n��������������������������������\n�\n������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������� \n�����������������������������������������������������������������������\n��\n���������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������� \n������������������������������������������������\n��\n������������������������������������������������������������������