raders violating the said law; \n(c) if so, the details thereof along with its current status;  \n(d) whether the profit earned from fo reign trade has registered an increase during the \npast few years; and \n(e) if so, the details thereof along with the steps taken or propos ed to be taken by the \nGovernment to increase India's foreign trade? \n \nANSWER \n \nokf.kT; ,oa m|ksx jkT; ea=h ¼Jherh fueZyk lhrkje.k½ ¼Lora= izHkkj½ \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COMMERCE AND INDUSTRY \n(INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \n (SMT. NIRMALA SITHARAMAN)  \n \n(a): Import of Toys is “free” subject to prescribed standards. Impo rt of Toys (all items \nunder EXIM Codes 9503 0010, 9503 0020, 9503 0030 and 9503 0090)  are permitted \nfreely when accompanied by the following two types of certificates: \n(i) A certificate that the toys bei ng imported conform to the stand ards prescribed \nin  \n(a) ASTM F963 or  \n(b) ISO 8124 (Parts I-III) or  \n(c) IS 9873 [Parts I-III] or  \n(d) EN 71;  \nand \n(ii) A Certificate of Conformance from the manufacturer that re presentative \nsample of the toys being imported have been tested by an indepe ndent \nlaboratory which is accredited under International Laboratory Accreditation \nCooperation (ILAC) Mutual Recognition Arrangement (MRA) and fou nd \nto meet the specifications indicated in (i) above. The certific ate would also \nlink the toys in the consignment to the period of manufacture i ndicated in \nthe Certificate of Conformity. \n\n \nFurther, the Bureau of Indian Standards (BIS) formulates standa rds applicable to domestic \nproducts and also mandates the us e of Standard Marks under a li cense which mutadis \nmutandis also apply to imported goods. Products have been notified unde r compulsory \ncertification / registration with one of the objectives of chec king influx of substandard \nproducts into Indian markets. Presently, there are 108 products  covered under Compulsory \nProduct Certification Scheme of BIS and 30 Electronic and IT Go ods under Compulsory \nRegistration Scheme of BIS. Till now, 204 licences have been is sued to Chinese \nmanufacturers for Steel products , Electrical products, Tyres &T ubes etc. as per Product \nCertification Scheme of BIS. A lso, 4636 manufacturers have been  granted registrations for \nElectronic and IT Goods as per Compulsory Registration Scheme of BIS \n \n(b) to (c) :  No such proposal is under consideration. \n(d):    The Government does not maintain data on profit and loss incurred in trade by private \nconcerns. \n(e):   To boost India’s exports, Foreign Trade Policy provides for duty neutralization schemes \nlike Export Promotion Capital Goods (EPCG) Scheme (duty free im ports of capital goods are \nallowed linked with export obligations) and Advance Authorisati on Scheme (duty free \nimports of raw materials, components, spares and consumables ar e allowed subject to export \nobligations within a fixed time period).  Moreover, there are i ncentive schemes like the \nMerchandise Exports from India Scheme (MEIS) (incentivizes expo rt of merchandise which \nare produced/manufactured in Indi a) and Services Exports from I ndia Scheme (SEIS) that \nincentivizes service exports. \n*** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|11|UNSTARRED |631", "t": "Inter-disciplinary Higher Education", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No.631 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2017 \n \nInter-disciplinary Higher Education \n \n631. SHRI NINONG ERING: \n Will the Minister of HUMAN RESOURC E DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has taken any initiatives to develop inter-disciplinary \neducation in higher education in the country; \n(b) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; and \n(c) the time by which it is likely to be set up? \n \nANSWER  \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(Dr. MAHENDRA NATH PANDEY) \n \n(a) to (c): The University Grants Commissi on (UGC) has introduced a Choice Based \nCredit System (CBCS) for all undergraduat e and postgraduate level degree, diploma \nand certificate programmes under the credit  system awarded by C entral, State and \nDeemed to be Universities. This system ensures an inter-disciplinary approach to \nlearning and enables students to  take up courses of their choice, learn at their own \npace, undergo additional courses and acquire more than the required credits. UGC has \nframed the guidelines on CBCS with a view to enhance academic standards and quality \nin higher education through innovation and improvements in curriculum, teaching-\nlearning process, examination and eval uation systems. The guidelines provide a \nframework for progressively adopting of t he different aspects of CBCS namely (i) \nsemester system; (ii) development of co re, elective and foundation courses; (iii) \nupdation of curricula utilizing the flexibilit y provided as per the guidelines; (iv) \nexamination and assessm ent system including awarding of  credits and credit transfer; \netc. UGC had circulated these guidelines to the Vice-Chancellors of all Universities and \nrequested them to introduce this system from the academic year 2015-16.  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|11|UNSTARRED |632", "t": "Model Schools", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION AND LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 632 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2017 \n \nModel Schools  \n \n632. SHRI SULTAN AHMED: \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:  \n(a) whether the Government has deferred/shel ved the proposal to set up Model Schools on \nPublic Private Partnership model in Non-Educationally Backward Blocks across the country; \n \n(b) if so, the reasons therefor; \n \n(c) the number of Model schools that have b ecome operational across the country including in \nthe State of West Bengal, State/UT-wise; and \n \n(d) the details of Model Schools approved and amount sanctioned in the State of West Bengal? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT  \n (SHRI UPENDRA KUSHWAHA) \n \n(a) & (b) : The Centrally Sponsored Scheme of Model School Scheme envisaged setting up of \none model school per block as a benchmark of excellence at th e block level. The scheme had \ntwo modes of implementation, viz. (i) setting up  of schools in educati onally backward blocks \n(EBBs) through State/UT Governments, and (ii)  establishing schools under Public-Private \nPartnership (PPP) mode in bloc ks which are not educationally  backward. However, the scheme \nhas been delinked from the support of the Gove rnment of India with effect from 2015-16 and \nstands transferred to States/UTs in the light of higher devoluti on of funds to the States as \nrecommended by the 14th Finance Commission. \n \n(c) : No schools have been aw arded under PPP mode. However, under State Sector Component \nof the Model School Scheme, the State/UT-wise details of model schools which have been made \noperational as on 31.3.2015 is given at Annexure-I. \n \n(d) :  A total of 67 model sch ools were approved in the State of West Bengal for which an \namount of Rs. 137.56 crore was sanctioned. De tailed year-wise status of model schools \napproved and amount sanctioned in the State of West Bengal is at Annexure-II. \n***** \n\nANNEXURE-I \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (c) OF THE LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO . 632 FOR 06.02.2017 BY  SHRI SULTAN AMHED  \nREGARDING MODEL SCHOOLS \n \nState/UT-wise details of model schools functional till the end of financial year 2014-15 \n \n \n \n \n  \nSl. No.  States Operational  Model \nSchools  \n1.  Andhra Pradesh 163 \n2.  Assam 14 \n3.  Chhattisgarh 74 \n4.  Gujarat 84 \n5.  Haryana 36 \n6.  Jharkhand 89 \n7.  Karnataka 74 \n8.  Madhya Pradesh 201 \n9.  Maharashtra 43 \n10.  Punjab 21 \n11.  Rajasthan 66 \n12.  Tamil Nadu 44 \n13.  Telangana 192 \n Total 1101 \n\nANNEXURE-II \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO  IN REPLY TO PART (d) OF THE LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO . 632 FOR 06.02.2017 BY  SHRI SULTAN AMHED  \nREGARDING MODEL SCHOOLS \n \n Model schools approved and amount sanctioned in the State of West Bengal \n \n \n \n \n  \n \n \nYear No. of Model Schools approved Amount sanctioned \n(Rs. in crore) \n2009-10 20 3.58 \n2010-11 - 19.07 \n2012-13 16 18.57 \n2014-15 31 96.34 \nTotal 67 137.56 "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|11|UNSTARRED |633", "t": "Single Teacher Schools", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION AND LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 633 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06th February, 2017  \n \nSingle Teacher Schools \n \n 633. KUMARI SUSHMITA DEV:  \n \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n (a) whether the Government is aware that there are a large number of schools in the \ncountry are run by a single teacher and if so, the details thereof; \n \n (b) the steps taken by the Government to ensure that schools have the prescribed student-\nteacher ratio; \n \n (c) whether it is a fact that the teachers of schools also perform duties other than \nteaching; \n \n (d) if so, the measures taken to ensure that the teachers whose primary job is to teach are \nable to fulfil their duties without the interruption all other duties allocated to them; \nand \n \n (e) the initiatives taken/proposed to be taken by the Government to fill vacancies of \nschool teachers in the country? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n (SHRI UPENDRA KUSHWAHA) \n(a):  As per Unified District Information System of Education (UDISE), 2015-16 \n(Provisional), there are 97923 single-teacher  Government schools at elementary and \nsecondary levels out of a total number of 11.05 lakh schools in the country. \n  \n \n(b) :  The recruitment, service conditions and redeployment of teachers are primarily in \nthe domain of respective State Government s and UT Administrations.  However, the \nCentral Government provides financial assistance to the State Governments and UT \nAdministrations for additional teachers under Sarva Shiksha Abhiyan (SSA) and \nRashtriya Madhyamik Shiksha Abhiyan (RMSA)  to maintain appropriate Pupil-Teacher \nRatio (PTR).  The PTR at National level for elementary schools is 25:1 and at  the \nsecondary it is 26:1 as per U-DISE 2015-16. The PTR at State and UT levels is also \nsatisfactory. It is therefore clear that there are enough teachers and the main issue is their \nunequal deployment. \n\n \n(c) & (d): As per the mandate given under Section 27 of the Right of Children to \nFree and Compulsory Education (RTE) Act, 2009, no teacher shall be deployed for any \nnon-educational purpose other than the decennial population census,  disaster relief duties \nor duties relating to elections to the local authority or State legislature or Parliament, as \nthe case may be. The Central Government has decided to undertake a study through \nNational University of Educational Planning and Administration (NUEPA) to assess time \nspent by teachers in non-teaching related activities and its adverse impact on education.  \n \n(e)  The Central Government has been consistently pursuing the matter of expeditious \nrecruitment and redeployment of teachers with States and UTs at various fora. \n***** \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|11|UNSTARRED |634", "t": "Scheme of Strengthening Education among Scheduled Tribes", "head": "  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TRIBAL AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 634 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2017 \n \nSCHEME OF STRENGTHENING EDUCATION AMONG  \nSCHEDULED TRIBES \n \n634. SHRI FEROZE VARUN GANDHI:  \n \nWill the Minister of TRIBAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government has increased/proposes to increase the level of funding \nfor the 'Scheme of Strengthening Education among Scheduled Tribes' owing to the \nincrease in number of districts having more than 25% ST girls population;  \n \n(b) if so, the details of funds allocated to these new districts since Caste Census data \nwas made public;  \n \n(c) whether the Government has increased the funding in consumerate with increase \nin population of STs during the last year; and  \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor along with the steps \ntaken/being taken in this regard? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF TRIBAL AFFAIRS \n(SHRI JASWANTSINH BHABHOR) \n \n(a) to (d): The relevant scheme being implemented through Voluntary \nOrganisations/Non-Governmental Organisations by the Ministry is called ‘ Strengthening \nEducation among Schedu led Tribe  (ST) G irls in Low Literacy Districts ’ and is being \nimplemented in 54 identified districts with 25% or more Scheduled Tribe population, and \nTribal female literacy rate below 35% or its fraction as per 2001 census  (Appendix I). \nAny other tribal block in a district, other than aforesaid 54 identified districts, which has \nScheduled Tribe population 25% or above, and tribal female literacy rate below 35% or \nits fractions, as per 2001 census, shall also be covered (Appendix-II, Blocks intimated \nby West Bengal and Karnataka so far. Such Blocks fulfilling the criteria in other States \nmay also be considered as and when reported).  In addition, the scheme will also cover \nareas below a Block level (e.g. Gram Panchayats) inhabited by the notified Primitive \nTribal Groups (PTGs) (Appendix-III). Out of all the aforesaid areas, the naxal affected \nareas shall be given priority. Thus, increase only in population of Scheduled Tribe girls \nin any district is not the criteria for funding under the scheme \n \n\n  \n Further, under the scheme , funds are not allocated to States or districts, but \nsanctioned to Non -Governmental Organisations/Voluntary Organisations for providing \nhostel facilities for tribal girls at the Block level to enable them to attend regular \nmiddle/secondary school, and at the panchayat level to attend regular primary school and  \nfor running of Educational Complexes for tribal girls, in the identified low literacy \ndistricts. Funds are sanctioned based on demand, recommendation of the State \nGovernment and subject to  fulfilment of all conditions of the scheme  and GFRs. \nMoreover, budget allocation is not available separately  for th is scheme, but for the \nscheme of ‘Grant-in-Aid to Voluntary Organisation Working for Welfare of Scheduled \nTribes’, which also includes this component. Hence, question of funding /increasing \nfunding to such new districts under the scheme does not arise. \nFunds released under the scheme during the last three years and the current \nfinancial year is as under:- \n FUND RELEASED DURING THE YEARS  2013-14 TO 2016-17 UNDER THE SCHEME \nOF 'STRENGTHENING EDUCATION AMONG SCHEDULED TRIBE GIRLS IN LOW \nLITERACY DISTRICTS \n  \n  \n(Rupees in Lakhs) \n2013-14   2014-15   2015-16   2016-17  (as on \n31.01.2017) \n4030.01 3500.00 5329.62 5576.42 \n \n  \n\n  \nAPPENDIX-I \nDISTRICTS IDENTIFIED FOR  THE  SCHEME  OF STRENGTHENING \nEDUCATION AMONG ST GIRLS IN LOW LITERACY DISTRICTS \nS.No.  \nName of State/Union \nTerritory \nName of Districts Percentage of \nscheduled tribe \npopulation in the \ndistrict as per \n2001 Census \nTribal Female \nLiteracy rate in the \nDistrict as per 2001 \nCensus \n1. Andhra Pradesh Khammam 26.47 27.6 \n2. Jammu & \nKashmir \nRajouri 33.12 26.0 \n3. Punch 39.99 28.3 \n4. Rajasthan Dungarpur 65.14 21.8 \n5. Banswara 72.27 18.9 \n6. Udaipur 47.86 25.7 \n7. Arunachal \nPradesh \nTawang 74.99 26.4 \n8. East Kameng 86.71 24.5 \n9. Lower Subansiri 90.09 33.4 \n10. Tirap 83.66 22.8 \n11. Nagaland Mon 93.92 35.4 \n12. Tripura Dhalai 54.02 33.3 \n13. Jharkhand Sahibganj 29.15 15.5 \n14. Pakaur 44.59 13.1 \n15. Dumka 39.89 21.7 \n16. Paschimi Singhbhum 53.36 23.7 \n17. Purbi Singhbhum 27.85 31.3 \n18. Lohardaga 55.70 32.0 \n19. Orissa Gajapati 50.78 14.8 \n20. Kandhamal 51.96 26.9 \n21. Nuapada 34.71 16.2 \n22. Kalahandi 28.65 17.2 \n23. Rayagada 55.76 10.1 \n24. Nabarangapur 55.03 11.1 \n25. Koraput 49.61 8.4 \n26. Malkangiri 57.43 7.5 \n27. Debgarh 33.60 31.2 \n28. Keonjhar 44.50 26.0 \n29. Mayurbhanj 56.60 23.5 \n30. Balangir 20.63 25.5 \n \n \n \n\n  \n \n31. Chhattisgarh Surguja 54.60 35.6 \n32. Korba 41.50 33.7 \n33. Bastar 66.31 23.1 \n34. Dantewada 78.51 13.4 \n35. Bilaspur 19.87 35.5 \n36. Madhya \nPradesh \nUmaria 44.05 30.4 \n37. Shehdol 44.48 31.0 \n38. Sidhi 29.89 21.6 \n39. Ratlam 25.89 27.7 \n40. Jhabua 86.85 19.4 \n41. Dhar 54.50 24.2 \n42. West Nimar 35.48 31.8 \n4"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|11|UNSTARRED |635", "t": "Setting up of Refilling Plant", "head": "LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 635 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 6th FEBRUARY 2017 \nSETTING UP OF REFILLING PLANT \n \n†635. SHRI JAI PRAKASH NARAYAN YADAV: \n \nपेट्रोलियम एवं प्राक ृ लिक गैस मंत्री \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state : \n \n(a) the number of LPG bottling plants of various Oil Marketing Companies (OMCs) operating \nin the country, OMCs/ location/State/UT-wise;  \n(b) whether the Government proposes to set up gas  refilling plant in various parts of the \ncountry including ceretain locations in Bihar; and  \n(c) if so, the details thereof and the time by which the supply of gas is likely to be done from \nthe said gas refilling plants, State/UT-wise? \n \nANSWER \n \nपेट्रोलियम एवं प्राक ृ लिक गैस मंत्रािय में राज्य मंत्री (स्विंत्र प्रभार) (श्री धमेन्द्र प्रधान) \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM & \nNATURAL GAS (SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n(a)  As on 01.10.2016, 188 LPG bottling plants of various Oil Marketing Companies \n(OMCs) are operating in the country. OMCs/locations/States/UTs -wise details of LPG \nbottling plants in the country are a