0.00 1.64 \nTamil Nadu 99.99 69.95 0.00 85.38 85.74 0.00 32.31 34.41 0.00 32.31 \nTripura 22.52 14.57 5.62 19.22 22.69 0.00 7.28 7.00 0.00 7.28 \nUP 181.38 176.08 0.00 154.87 165.45 3.78 58.59 69.99 0.00 58.59 \nUttarakhand 9.67 10.81 2.08 8.25 8.81 0.00 3.12 3.74 0.00 4.68 \nWest Bengal 82.96 56.24 6.00 70.84 47.40 0.00 26.80 3.60 0.00 26.80 \nTelangana 0.00 0.00 0.00 42.86 45.79 0.00 16.22 15.47 0.85 16.22 \nTotal 1610.35 # 1244.28 89.08 1375.01# 1291.63 32.51 520.24^ 458.54 29.75 521.8 ^ \n\n \n \nPAGE 1/1 OF ANNEXURE-II \nL.S.US.Q.NO. 775 FOR 7.2.2017 \n \n \nStatement of allocation and release of f"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|11|UNSTARRED |776", "t": "Integrated Farming in KVKs", "head": "1 \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURAL RESEARCH & EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 776 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 07/02/2017 \n \nINTEGRATED FARMING IN KVKs \n \n776. SHRI PRALHAD JOSHI: \n   \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE    \nd`f\"k ,oa fdlku dY;k.k ea=h be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has a proposal to start integrated farming in one hundred \nKrishi Vigyan Kendras (KVKs); \n \n(b) if so, the details of the KVKs identified for the  said purpose along with the \nobjectives sought to be achieved by the said project; and \n \n(c) the other steps taken by the Government to modernize and improve the functioning \nof KVKs in order to assist the farmers in a better way? \n \nA N S W E R \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nd`f”k ,oa fdlku dY;k.k ea=ky; esa jkT; ea=h \n(SHRI S.S. AHLUWALIA) \n \n(a) & (b): Yes, Madam. The Indian Council of Agricultural Research (ICAR) is \nimplementing a project for establishment of demonstra tion units on Integrated Farming \nSystem (IFS) in 100 Krishi Vigyan Kendras (KVKs). State wise list of KVKs involved in \nthe project is given in Annexure-I. The objective of setting up of demonstration units on \nIFS in KVKs is to provide training to farmers a nd demonstrate them the benefits of \nintegrated farming practices. \n \n(c) During the XII Plan , selected existing KVKs have been provided with additional \nfacilities like Rain Water Harvesting Structure with Micro Irrigation System, Soil and \nWater Testing facil ity, Minimal Processing facility, Carp Hatchery facility, Integrated \nFarming System (IFS) and e-Extension facility.     \n \nSimilarly, selected KVKs have been provided new facilities viz.  Technology \nInformation Unit, Mini Seed Processing facility,  Provision o f 25 KVA silent Genset,  \nMicro Nutrient Analysis facility, Solar panel facility, establishment of vKVK and KVK \nNet, e-Farmers facility,  Specialized KVKs, Mini Soil Testing labs and Pulses Seed Hubs.  \n \n      The number of existing KVKs considered for modern izing with additional facilities \nand new facilities during XII Plan is given at Annexure-II. \n******* \n\n2 \n \n \n \nAnnexure-I \n[Part (a) & (b) to Lok Sabha USQ No.776 for 07-02-2017] \n \n \nState wise list of KVKs for establishment of climate resilient  \nIntegrated Farming System Demonstration units \n \nState Districts \nA&N Islands (1 KVK) Port Blair \nAndhra Pradesh (5 KVKs) Anantapur (Reddipalli), Viajynagaram, Nellore, Guntur \n(LAM) and Chittoor (Kalikiri) \nArunachal Pradesh (1 KVK) East Siang \nAssam (2 KVKs) Dibrugarh and Barpeta \nBihar (6 KVKs) Darbhanga, East Champaran, Vaishali, Supaul, Arwal and \nBhagalpur  \nChhattisgarh (4 KVKs) Jashpur, Raigarh, Kawardha and Korea \nGujarat (4 KVKs) Banaskantha, Sabarkantha, Amreli and Narmada \nHaryana  (4 KVKs) Faridabad, Mahendergarh, Gurgaon and Karnal \nHimachal Pradesh (2 KVKs) Hamirpur and Mandi \nJammu & Kashmir (3 KVKs) Kathua, Srinagar and Pulwama \nJharkhand (3 KVKs) Jamtara, Saraikela-Kharsawan and Gumla \nKarnataka (3 KVKs) Bidar, Tumkur and Bijapur \nKerala (2 KVKs) Calicut and Alleppey \nMadhya Pradesh (5 KVKs) Dewas, Gwalior, Mandla, Neemuch and Ujjain \nMaharashtra (3 KVKs) Dhule, Nagpur and Ratnagiri \nManipur (1 KVK) Imphal East \nMeghalaya (2 KVKs) Ribhoi and East Khasi Hills \nMizoram (2 KVKs) Serchipp and Mamit \nNagaland (2 KVKs) Wokha and Zunheboto \nOdisha (5 KVKs) Sundergarh, Boudh, Dhenkanal, Ganjam-II, and \nJagatsinghpur  \nPunjab (4 KVKs) Bathinda, Barnala, Hoshiarpur and Patiala \nRajasthan (8 KVKs)  Bikaner-I, Alwar-I, Dausa, Swai Madhopur, Nagaur, Pali, \nTonk and Jodhpur-I  \nSikkim (1 KVK) North Sikkim \nTamil Nadu (5 KVKs) Dharmapuri, Salem, Sivagangai, Cuddalore and Vellore  \nTelangana State (4 KVKs) Khammam, Mahaboobnagar  (Palem), Warangal (Mamnoor) \nand Karimnagar \nTripura (1 KVK) West Tripura \nUttar Pradesh (13 KVKs) \nGhazipur, Lalitpur, Ghaziabad, Fatehpur, Kushinagar, \nLucknow, Bulandshahar, Deoria, Baghpat, Chandauli, \nPilibhit, Mirzapur and Varanasi \nUttarakhand (1 KVK) Bageshwar \nWest Bengal (3 KVKs) Darjeeling, North 24 Parganas and South 24 Parganas \n(Nimpith) \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n3 \n \n \nAnnexure-II \n[Part (c) to Lok Sabha USQ No.776 for 07-02-2017] \n \nNumber of existing KVKs considered for upgrading with additional facilities and new \nfacilities during XII Plan. \n \nS.No. Additional facilities No. of KVKs covered \nduring 12th Plan till \nMarch 2017 \n1 Rain Water Harvesting Structure 18 \n2 Soil and Water Testing Laboratory 39 \n3 Minimal Processing Facility  25 \n4 Carp Hatchery 19 \n5 Integrated Farming System units 281 \n6 e-linkage Facility 97 \n   \n  S.No. New Initiatives No. of KVKs covered \nduring 12th Plan till \nMarch 2017 \n1 Technology Information Unit 211 \n2 Mini Seed Processing Facility 3 \n3 Provision of 25 KVA Silent Genset  83 \n4 Micro Nutrient Analysis Facility 1 \n5 Solar Panel 15 \n6 v KVK and KVK Net 75 \n7 e-Farmers 92 \n8 Specialized KVKs 7 \n9 Mini Soil Testing Labs 620 \n10 "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|11|UNSTARRED |777", "t": "Assistance for Crop Loss by Rural Cooperatives", "head": " \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nO.I.H\n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE,\n \nCOOPERATION\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. \n7\n77\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON \nTHE \n7\nTH\n \nFEBRUARY\n, \n201\n7\n \n \nASSISTANCE FOR CROP LOSS BY RURAL\n \nC\nOOPERATIVES\n \n \n \n777. \n \nSHRI HARINARAYAN\n \nRAJBHAR:\n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n  \n \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं \u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण \nमं\u0000ी\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether the rural cooperative\n \ncommittees are providing financial\n \nassistance/crop loans to \nthe farmers\n \nsuffering crop loss;\n \n \n(b) \n \nif so, the details thereof during the\n \nlast three years, State\n-\nwise incl\nuding\n \nUttar Pradesh?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं \n\u0000कसान \nक\u0000याण \nमं\u0000ालय म\u0000 रा\u0000य मं\u0000ी\n \n \n   \n(SHRI PARSHOTTAM  RUPALA)\n \n \n(a):\n \n \nNo, Madam.\n \n \n(b):\n \nDoes not arise.\n \n \n \n \n********\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|11|UNSTARRED |778", "t": "Rapid Action Force Battalion of CRPF", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 778  \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 7TH FEBRUARY, 2017 / MAGHA 18, 1938 \n(SAKA) \n \nRAPID ACTION FORCE BATTALION OF CRPF \n \n778.  SHRI CHANDRA PRAKASH JOSHI: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether Rapid Action Force  Battalions of CRPF have been located in  \nseveral States; \n \n(b) if so, whether Rajasthan has  submitted a proposal for establishment \nof a Rapid Action Force Battalion of CRPF in the State; and \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof and the time  by which such Battalion is \nlikely to be established in Rajasthan? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KIREN RIJIJU) \n \n(a): Yes. The Rapid Action Force Battalions of CRPF have been located \nin various States.   \n \n(b) to (c):  A request was received from Government of Rajasthan for \nestablishment of an RAF Battalion in the State.  Government has agreed \nto the proposal. Order has be en issued on 8.12.2016.  Proposal for \nsanction of manpower for the RAF Battalion is under consideration. \n \n \n******* "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|11|UNSTARRED |779", "t": "Acid Attack", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 779  \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 7TH FEBRUARY, 2017 / MAGHA 18, 1938 \n(SAKA) \n \nACID ATTACK \n \n779.  SHRIMATI RAKSHATAI KHADSE: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether as per the data of the National Crime Records Bureau \n(NCRB), the figures relating to acid attacks is the highest in West \nBengal, Uttar Pradesh and NCT of Delhi; \n \n(b) whether the Government proposes to frame stringent regulations on \nacid retailing and whole selling in the country; \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(d) the other corrective steps taken by the Government in this regard? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI HANSRAJ GANGARAM AHIR) \n \n(a): As per the information provided by the National Crime Records \nBureau (NCRB), a total of 55 cases in Uttar Pradesh, 39 cases in West \nBengal and 21 cases in Delhi UT were registered under acid attacks \n(section 326A IPC) during 2015, contributing 24.8%, 17.6% and 9.5% o f \ntotal such cases registered in the country respectively, which are \nhighest in the country. \n.....2/ \n\n \n-2- \nL.S.US.Q.NO. 779 FOR 7.2.2017 \n(b) to (d): As per the seventh schedule to the Constitution of India \n`Police` and `Public Order` are State subjects and as such, the primary \nresponsibility of prevention, detection, registration, investigation and \nprosecution of crime, lies with the State Governments/  Union Territory \nAdministrations.  \nMinistry of Home Affairs has issued a detailed Advisory in accordance \nwith the direction of the Hon’ble Supreme Court, in WP (Crl) 129 of 2006, \nto all the States/UTs titled ‘Measures to be taken to prevent acid attacks \non people and for treatment and rehabilitation of survivors’ dated 30th \nAugust, 2013 and another Advisory dated 20th April, 2015 on expediting \ncases of Acid Attack on women. These advisories are available on \nwww.mha.nic.in. \n \n******* "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|11|UNSTARRED |78", "t": "Driving Licence and Fitness Certificate Fee", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, \nCOOPERATION\n \n& FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n \n78\n \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE \n7\nTH\n \nFEBRUARY, 2017\n \n \n \nINTELLECTUAL PROPERTY RIGHTS IN AGRICULTURE\n \n \n*78. \n    \nSHRI B. VINOD KUMAR: \n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS \nWELFARE   \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \n½ãâ¨ããè\n  \n \nbe pleased to state\n:\n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether the Government has constituted the Protection of Plant Varieties and \nFarmers Rights Authority (PPV&\nFRA) to implement Intellectual Property Rights (IPR) in \nagriculture for the preservation of plants species and to promote the new species of plants \nas well as rights of the farmers and if so, the details thereof; and \n \n \n(b) \n \nwhether\n \nthe Government proposes to reconstitute the said authority and if so, the \ndetails thereof?\n \nANSWER\n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n  \n½ãâ¨ããè\n \n \n \n \n  \n(SHRI RADHA MOHAN SINGH\n)\n \n \n \n(a) & (b\n):\n \nA statement is laid on the Table of the\n \nHouse.\n \n \n \n \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN\n \nREPLY TO PARTS (a) &\n \n(\nb\n) OF L\nOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. \n \n78\n  \nDUE FOR REPLY ON \n7\nTH\n \nFEBRUARY, 2017\n.\n \n \n(a): \n \nYes, Madam.  The Department of Agriculture, Cooperation and Farmers Welfare \nhas established the Protection of \nPlant Varieties and Farmers’ Rights Authority \n(PPV&FRA) under Section 3 of the Protection of Plant Varieties and Farmers’ Rights \nAct, 2001 (PPV&FR Act, 2001) on 11\nth\n \nNovember, 2005 to implement Intellectual \nProperty Rights in agriculture and for the preser\nvation of plants species and to promote \nthe development of new plant varieties as well as to protect the rights of the farmers.  \nThe Authority shall also facilitate the growth of Seed Industry in the country to make \navailable quality seed\ns\n \nand planting mat\nerials to the farmers.  The PPV&\nFR Authority\n \nconsists of a Chairperson and fifteen members.  Out of fifteen members, there are eight \nex\n-\nofficio members and seven non\n-\nofficial members. \n \n \n(b):\n \nThere is no proposal to re\n-\nconstitute the Protection of Plant Var\nieties and \nFarmers’ Rights Authority at present. \n \n \n*****\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|11|UNSTARRED |780", "t": "Organic Chemical Industry", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CHEMICALS & FERTILIZERS \nDEPARTMENT OF CHEMICALS & PETROCHEMICALS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 780 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 07.02.2017 \n \nOrganic Chemical Industry \n \n780:   SHRI C. R. PATIL \n \n Will the Minister of CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS be pleased to state:- \n \na) whether the Government has any plan to promote the organic chemical \nindustry in the country; and  \nb) if so, the details thereof ? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE OF CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS, SHIPPING, ROAD \nTRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS (SHRI MANSUKH L. MANDAVIYA) \n \n \n(a)   to (b)  The Chemical sector is delicensed and decontrolled. Organic chemical \nIndustry is an integral part of the overall chemical sector. The entrepreneurs are \nsetting up units in the private sector based on techno econo mic feasibility, demand \nand supply scenario and cost of feedstock/raw materials.  The Government has \ntaken various steps including rationalization of custom duty on the feedstock/building \nblocks for having synergy in the complete value chain for boosting the chemical \nsector and competiveness of the industry in the country . The various \nseminars/workshops including ‘INDIA-CHEM GUJARAT 2015’ and ‘INDIA -CHEM, \nMumbai 2016’ etc. were held for exchange of technology, ideas and innovations for \ngrowth in the Chemical sector. \n**** \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|11|UNSTARRED |781", "t": "Purchase of Paddy", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \nDEPARTMENT OF FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 781 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 07th FEBRUARY, 2017 \n \nPURCHASE OF PADDY \n \n781.  SHRI RAM TAHAL CHOUDHARY \n \nWill the Minister of CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC \nDISTRIBUTION miHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k ea=h   be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the farmers in the State of  Jharkhand have suffered \nsubstantial lossesue  to non -purchase procurement of paddy  at Minimum \nSupport Price during the year  2015-16 as there were no purchase  \nprocurement centres in the State; \n \n(b)  if so, the reasons therefor along with the response of the Government \nthereto; and \n \n(c)  the special ef forts made to set up  purchase procurement centres for \npaddy and to procure maximum quantity of paddy  at Minimum Support Price \nin coming years? \n \n \nA N S W E R \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR  CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n(SHRI C. R. CHAUDHARY) \n(a), (b) & (c) No, Madam. Food Corporation of India along with State \nGovernment Agencies had opened 455 purchase centres in Jharkhand \nRegion for smooth procurement of paddy during Kharif Marketing Season \n(KMS) 2015 -16. Furthermore, private players were also engage d for \nprocurement operations for enhancing the outreach of Minimum Support \nPrice to farmers. During KMS 2014 -15 and 2015 -16 a total quantity of 0.02 \nLakh Metric Ton (LMT) and 2.06 LMT of rice respectively was procured in \nJharkhand Region. The number of Pro curement Centres operated during \nKMS 2014-15 and 2015-16 are as under:-  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n**** \n KMS 2014-15 KMS 2015-16 \nFCI 10 25 \nState agency 886 229 \nPrivate player 00 201 \nTotal  896 455 "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|11|UNSTARRED |782", "t": "SOPs for Good Samaritans", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 782  \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 7TH FEBRUARY, 2017 / MAGHA 18, 1938 \n(SAKA) \n \nSOPS FOR GOOD SAMARITANS \n \n782.  SHRI BAIJAYANT JAY PANDA: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government is aware that as per the judgement in Writ \nPetition (C) No. 235 of 2012 for the protection of Good Samaritans, \nStandard Operating Procedures (SOPs) have been laid down for \nexamination of Good Samaritans by the police; \n \n(b) if so, whether any steps have been taken to ensure that every police \nofficer is made aware of the SOPs; \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(d) if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI HANSRAJ GANGARAM AHIR) \n \n(a) to ( d):   Ministry of Road Transport & Highways has notified the \nguidelines on Good Samaritans on 12 th May 2015 and the  Standard \nOperating Procedures (SOPs)  on the Good Samaritans on 21 st  January \n2016.     Since  “Police”  is  a  State  subject,  the  Home  Secretaries of  \n.....2/ \n\n \n-2- \nL.S.US.Q.NO.782 FOR 7.2.2017  \n \nStates/UTs have been advised on the same vide Ministry of Home \nAffairs letters dated 16.06.2015 and 13.04.2016 respectively.  The \nguidelines and the Notification issued by Ministry of Road Transport & \nHighways are also available on the website of Ministry of Road \nTransport & Highways viz http://morth.nic.in/. \n \n \n******* "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|11|UNSTARRED |783", "t": "Waste Management", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE  \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 783 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 07.02.2017 \n \nWaste Management \n \n783. SHRI SHIVKUMAR UDASI:  \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) whether the quantum of waste and solid waste generation has increased in the cities of \nthe country and if so, the details thereof; \n(b) whether most of the Indian cities do not possess any waste management system and if \nso, the details thereof; \n(c) whether according to experts, India is following a flawed system of waste disposal and \nmanagement and if so, the reaction of the Government thereto; and \n(d) whether the Government is considering to reinvent waste management and if so, the \ndetails thereof? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND \nCLIMATE CHANGE  \n(SHRI ANIL MADHAV DAVE) \n(a):  As per Annual Reports submitted by State Pollution Control Boards (SPCBs) /Pollution \nControl Committees (PCCs) till December 2016, the quantity of solid waste generation is \nestimated to be about 1.43 l akh tons per day in the year 2015 -16. There has been increase in \nquantum of waste generation in cities of the country with the increase of population. \n \n(b): The Municipal Solid Wastes (Management and Handling) Rules, 2000 vest the responsibility \nof sanitation and waste management  with municipal authorities.  However, inadequacy in \nimplementation of these Rules has been noticed  on account of growing urban population, \nunplanned civic infrastructure, weak financial  base and enforcement mechanism in most of the \nmunicipal authorities.  \n \n(c) &(d): The regime for ma nagement and disposal of solid waste is currently governed by t he \nSolid Waste Management Rules, 2016, which have been evolved in 2016  after consultation with \nstakeholders.  The new Rules provide ways and means of setting up self sustainable solid waste \nmanagement system by the local bodies. The generators  of waste have been mandated to \nsegregate waste at source. The l ocal bodies are required to collect segregated waste and \nchannelize it to waste processing facilities for recycling, recovery and reuse.  \n*** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|11|UNSTARRED |784", "t": "Closure of HMT Units", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HEAVY INDUSTRIES AND PUBLIC ENTERPRISES \nDEPARTMENT OF HEAVY INDUSTRY \n \nLOK SABHA \n UNSTARRED QUESTION NO.784 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 07.02.2017 \n \nClosure of HMT Units \n \n784. SHRI RAJAN VICHARE: \n \nWill the Minister of HEAVY INDUSTRIES AND PUBLIC ENTERPRISES be pleased \nto state: \n    \n(a) whether the Government has decided to close down the three unviable HMT Units and if so, \nthe details thereof;  \n(b) whether the Government has also decided to stop manufacturing watches and if so, the details \nthereof; \n(c) whether the employees of the three unviable units of HMT are likely to be offered an \nattractive Voluntary Retirement Scheme (VRS) linked to 2007 pay scales; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n   \nANSWER \n MINISTER  OF  STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF  HEAVY INDUSTRIES AND \nPUBLIC ENTERPRISES (SHRI  BABUL SUPRIYO) \n \n(a): Yes Madam. The Cabinet Committee on Economic Affairs (CCEA) in its meeting held on \n6th January 2016 has approved the closure of HMT Watches Limited, HMT Chinar Watches \nLimited and HMT Bearings Limited and relieving all the employees in terms of approved \nRoadmap for CPSEs under DHI by offering an attractive VRS package to willing employees and \nby retrenchment under Industrial Dispute Act, 1947 of those not opting for VRS. \nAll employees of HMT Chinar Watches Limited and HMT Bearings Limited as well as, 813 \nemployees of HMT Watches Limited who opted for VRS have been relieved and compensation \npaid.   \n  \n(b): Yes Madam.   The manufacturing of watches  at HMT Watches Limited and HMT Chinar \nWatches Limited has been stopped. \n \n(c) & (d): Yes Madam.  All employees of HMT Watches Limited, HMT Chinar Watches Limited \nand HMT Bearings Limited have been offered attractive Voluntary Retirement Scheme (VRS) \npackage on 2007 notional pay scale (as against salaries in 1992 pay scales being paid to them) \nwith gratuity and leave encashment also at 2007 notional pay scales, in relaxation of DPE \nguidelines. \n \n********** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|11|UNSTARRED |785", "t": "Setting up of Fishing Harbours", "head": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF ANIMAL HUSBANDRY, DAIRYING AND FISHERIES \n LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.785 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 7TH FEBRUARY, 2017 \n \nSETTING UP OF FISHING HARBOURS \n \n785.   SHRI P.K. BIJU: \n                     \n    Will the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE कृषि और ककसान कल्याण मंत्री \n be pleased to state: \n \n(a)     whether the Government proposes to set up new fishing harbours in Kerala under the \nCentrally Sponsored Schemes to enhance the livelihoods of fishermen and if so, the details \nthereof;  \n(b)    whether there is any special assistance component in the central plan for the fishermen; \nand \n(c)    if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n       (SHRI SUDARSHAN BHAGAT) \n \n(a):  Yes, Madam. The Department of Animal Husbandry, Dairying and  Fisheries (DADF),  \nMinistry of Agriculture and Farmers Welfare under the Central Plan Scheme on Blue \nRevolution: Integrated Development and Management of Fisheries  provides financial assistance \nto the coastal State Governments including Kerala and Union Te rritories for setting up of fishing \nharbours, based on the techno -economic feasibility reports, viability and complete proposals \nsubmitted by them. The admissible central financial assistance under Blue Revolution scheme \nfor construction of fishing harbour s for the State of Kerala is 50 percent of the approved project \ncost.  \n(b) & (c): The Central Plan Scheme ‘Blue Revolution: Integrated Development and \nManagement of Fisheries’ being implemented by the DADF aims for an integrated development \nand management of the fisheries sector covering inland fisheries, aquaculture, marine fisheries, \nmariculture, cage/pen culture and creation of fisheries infrastructure .  The scheme has various \nbroad components namely, (i)  Development of Inland Fisheries and Aquaculture, (ii) \nDevelopment of Marine Fisheries, Infrastructure and Post Harvest Operations, (iii) National \nFisheries Development Board (NFDB) and its activities , (iv) National Scheme of Welfare of \nFishermen, (v) Strengthening of Database and Geographical Information System of the Fisheries \nSector and (vi) Monitoring, Control and Surveillance (MCS) and other need based interventions. \nAdmissible central financial assistance under this scheme is provided to the eligible fishers, fish \nfarmers and others through the concerned State Government and UT Administrations.  \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|11|UNSTARRED |786", "t": "FPI in Hill States", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 786 \nANSWERED ON 7TH FEBRUARY, 2017  \n \nFPI IN HILL STATES \n \n786. SHRI VIRENDER KASHYAP \n  \nWill the Minister of FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government proposes to promote Food Processing Industries (FPI) in hill \nareas of the country; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) whether the Government has any plan to establish such industries in tomato and other \nseasonal vegetable growing areas; and \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES \n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI) \n \n(a) &  (b): Yes, Madam. The Ministry is implementing various Central Sector Schemes for \npromotion and development of food processing sector in the country including hill areas namely \n(i) Scheme for Mega Food Park; (ii) Scheme for Modernization of Abattoirs; (iii ) Scheme for \nIntegrated Cold Chain and Value Addition Infrastructure; (iv) Scheme for Creation/ Expansion \nof Food Processing and Preservation Capacities; (v) Scheme for Quality Assurance; and (vi) \nScheme for Human Resources and Institutions. Under these sc hemes, higher level of financial \nassistance as grant -in-aid is provided for the projects set up in difficult and hilly areas (North -\nEast States including Sikkim, Jammu & Kashmir, Himachal Pradesh and Uttarakhand).  The \ndetailed guidelines of these schemes,  explaining the pattern of financial assistance, eligibility \ncriteria, procedure for approval of projects / release of grant -in-aid are available on the \nMinistry’s website at www.mofpi.nic.in.  \n \n(c) & (d): Under the schemes of the Ministry of Food Processing Industries, projects may be set \nup throughout the country including the tomato and other seasonal vegetable growing areas.  \nProposals for setting up of the projects are invited from the eligible entrepreneurs and entities \nand eligible proposals are selected based upon the criteria laid down in the respective Scheme \nguidelines. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|11|UNSTARRED |787", "t": "Grants to check Pollution", "head": "OIH \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.787 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 07.02.2017 \n \nGrants to check Pollution \n \n 787 SHRI LAXMI NARAYAN YADAV: \n  \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has released any funds to Madhya Pradesh to check \npollution in the State; \n(b)  if so, the details thereof during each of the last three years; and \n(c)  the details of the pollution control related works carried out from this fund during the said \nperiod? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND \nCLIMATE CHANGE  \n(SHRI ANIL MADHAV DAVE) \n \n(a) to (c) Details of f unds released to Madhya Pradesh to check pollution and address \nother environmental issues under the National Plan for Conservation of Aquatic Eco- Systems \n(NPCA), National Ambient Air Monitoring Programme (NAMP) and National Water \nMonitoring Programme (NWMP) during the last three years an d the current year are given in \nAnnexure. \n \nActivities carried out from these funds include de -weeding, sewerage network, sewage \ntreatment plant, de -silting, storm water drain , low cost sanitation, lake front development, \nmonitoring of waste water quality and operation of air and water quality monitoring stations.  \n \n***** \n\nANNEXURE  \nANNEXURE REFERRED IN REPLY TO THE LOK SABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 787 DUE FOR \nANSWER ON 07.02.2017 REGARDING \" GRANTS TO CHECK POLLUTION \" RAISED BY SHRI LAXMI \nNARAYAN YADAV, HON’BLE MEMBER OF PARLIAMENT \n \n \nFunds released to Madhya Pradesh u nder the National Plan for Conservation \nof Aquatic Eco- Systems (NPCA), National Ambient Air Monitoring Programme \n(NAMP) and National Water Monitoring Programme (NWMP) during the last \nthree years and the current year: \n \n \nS. \nNo. \nScheme / \nProgramme \nFunds released to Madhya Pradesh (Rs. in Lakh) \n2013-14 2014-15 2015-16 2016-17 \n1. NPCA - 250.00 490.00 1200.00 \n2. NAMP - - - 79.84 \n3. NWMP 18.99 - 6.71 - \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|11|UNSTARRED |788", "t": "Arsenic Contamination in Water", "head": "OIH \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.788 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 07.02.2017 \n \nArsenic Contamination in Water \n \n788. SHRI AJAY MISRA TENI: \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)  whether the levels of arsenic contamination have reached alarming proportions in \nmajor rivers across the country including Ganga and Yamuna due to disposal of \nindustrial waste and residual ashes from the coal based power plants; \n(b)  if so, whether the Government has conducted any study to examine the ill effects of \nhigh levels of arsenic contamination in water; \n(c)  if so, the details and findings thereof; \n(d)  whether arsenic adversely affects human brains, nervous system and pregnancy; and \n(e)  if so, the measures being taken by the Government to reduce the increasing levels of \narsenic contamination in water? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND \nCLIMATE CHANGE  \n(SHRI ANIL MADHAV DAVE) \n \n(a)to (d) As per information received from the Central Pollution Control Board which \nmonitors the water quality of river s Ganga and Yamuna, surface water of the \ntwo rivers does not indicate the presence of a rsenic in aqueous phase . The \nstudies carried out by the Central Ground Water Board (CGWB) in respect of \nground water quality monitoring  indicate that groundwater is contaminated \nwith arsenic in parts of 153 districts in 2 1 States and UTs. Long-term \nexposure to arsenic in drinking water may cause several health implications. \n  \n(e)  Remedial measures undertaken in arsenic contaminated areas include sharing \nof ground water quality analysis data by the CGWB with concerned State \nagencies for awareness and necessary action; exploratory drilling carried out \nfor tapping contamination free aquifers in the contamination affected areas ; \nand undertaking the National Aquifer Mapping Programme in the XII\nth plan \nincluding construction of tube wells for tapping arsenic safe deeper aquifer in \nthe States of Uttar Pradesh, Bihar, Jharkhand and West Bengal.  \n \n***** \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|11|UNSTARRED |789", "t": "Pollution by Coal Mining", "head": "OIH \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.789 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 07.02.2017 \n \nPollution by Coal Mining \n \n789. SHRIMATI REKHA VERMA: \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate:  \n \n(a)  whether the Government is aware that the production of coal accounts for the \nsubstantial rise of dust particles in the air and the menace of water pollution; and  \n(b)  if so, the measures taken by the Government for processing of dust produced by coal \nmining and for recycling of water? \n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND \nCLIMATE CHANGE  \n(SHRI ANIL MADHAV DAVE) \n \n(a) Activities related to production of coal such as drilling, blasting, extraction, \ntransportation and crushing may lead to rise of dust particles in air and water pollution. \n \n(b) The measures taken by the Government for mitigation  of dust produced by coal mining \nand for recycling of water include use of Automatic, Mobile and Fixed Water Sprinklers, Wet \nDrilling, Side Discharge Loaders, Load Haul Dumpers and Conveyors, Plantation, Effluent \nTreatment Plants, Sewage Treatment Plants, R euse/recycle of water and construction of \nGarland Drain around overburden dumps terminating at sedimentation ponds to arrest flow of \nsilt to the rivers/nalas.       \n******* "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|11|UNSTARRED |790", "t": "Indiscriminate use of Plastics", "head": "OIH \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE  \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 790 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 07.02.2017 \n \nIndiscriminate use of Plastics \n \n790. SHRI SUKHBIR SINGH JAUNAPURIA:  \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) whether the Government has fixed any criteria for manufacturing and usage of non-\ndegradable plastic;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof;  \n(c) whether indiscriminate use of plastic is one of the major causes of environmental \ndegradation; and  \n(d)  if so, the remedial steps proposed to be taken by the Government in this regard? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND \nCLIMATE CHANGE  \n(SHRI ANIL MADHAV DAVE) \n(a) & (b): The Ministry  has notified the Plastic Waste Management (PWM) Rules, 2016, which \nspecify responsibilities of State Governments and Union Territory administrations, local bodies, \ngenerators of waste, producers or brand owners in terms of implementation of provisions for \nextended producer responsibility, recyclers of plastic waste, street vendors and retailers.  T he \nRules also regulate manufacturing, sale, distribution and use of carry bags made of pl astics and \nplastic sheets or like or cover made of plastic sheet and multilayered packaging. \n \n(c)&(d): The  plastic waste when disposed off on soil and in landfill sites without adequate \narrangements for collection and treatment has the potential of adversely affecting the soil, ground \nwater and the surroundings. State Governments have been advised to ensure effective \nimplementation of the PWM Rules, 2016.  The Centre Poll ution Control Board has i ssued \ndirections to Municipal Commissioners  and/or Executive Officers of 46 million -plus cities, 20 \nState Capitals , and 112 towns situated on the banks of river Ganga regarding closure of \nunauthorized plastic manufacturing industri es, regulating thickness of plastic carry  bags and \nsetting-up of plastic waste management system as per the Rules . Steps for creating awareness \nregarding use and sell of plastic carry bags not less than ‘ 50 microns’ and other issues related to \nplastic waste management have also been taken. \n   \n*** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|11|UNSTARRED |791", "t": "Kidnapping of Women and Children", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. †791 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 7TH FEBRUARY, 2017/ MAGHA 18, 1938 (SAKA) \n \nKIDNAPPING OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN \n \n†791. SHRI PANKAJ CHAUDHARY: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether a large number of cases of  kidnapping of women and children \nhave been reported in the country; \n \n (b) if so, the details thereof alongwith  the total number of such cases \nreported, number of women and children traced/untraced, separately \nduring each of the last three years and the current year, State-wise; \n \n(c) the action taken against the organized  gangs who trap the \nchildren/minors for flesh  trade and child labour along with the gangs  \nbusted during the said period, State-wise; \n \n(d) whether the Government has  identified the reasons behind in such \ncrimes increase and if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(e) the details of advisories/guidelines  issued to the states in this regard \nand the  other corrective steps taken by the  Government to stop such \ncases in future? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI HANSRAJ GANGARAM AHIR) \n \n(a) & (b): As per the information provided by the National Crime Records \nBureau (NCRB),  a total  51,881 cases in 2013, 57,311 cases in 2014 and \n59,277 cases in 2015 were registered under  kidnapping  and  abduction of  \n...2/- \n\n-2- \nLS.US.Q.No.791 FOR 07.02.2017 \n women. A total of 38,619 women in 2014 and 44,666 women in 2015 were \nrescued. A total of 28,167 cases in 2013, 37,854 cases in 2014 and 41,893 \ncases in 2015  were registered under kidnapping and abduction of \nchildren. A total of 27,473 children in 2014 and 32,651 children  in 2015 \nwere rescued.  The Bureau has started collecting data on victims \nrecovered/unrecovered from kidnapping & abduction since 2014 only . \nState/UT-wise data is enclosed as Annexure-I, II & III.  \n(c) to (e): As per the seventh schedule to the Constitution of India \n`Police` and `Public Order` are State subjects and as s uch, the primary \nresponsibility of prevention, detection, registration, investigation and \nprosecution of crime, lies with the State Governments/ Union Territory \nAdministrations. Ministry of Home Affairs has issued Advisories dated 14 th \nJuly, 2010 regarding  “Crime against children”, dated 25 th June, 2013 \nregarding “Hon’ble Supreme Court’s direction to file FIR in case of Missing \nChildren” and dated 12 th May, 2015 regarding “Comprehensive approach \ntowards crimes against women”. These advisories are also avail able on \nwww.mha.nic.in. \n \n \n\n                 \nAnnexure- I \n \nState/UT-wise cases registered (CR), persons arrested (PAR), cases charge sheeted (CS), persons charge sheeted (PCS), cases convicted (CV) and persons convicted (PCV) \nunder kidnapping & abduction of women during 2013-2015 \nSL State/UT \n2013 2014 2015 \nCR PAR CS PCS CV PCV CR PAR CS PCS CV PCV CR PAR CS PCS CV PCV \n1 Andhra Pradesh 1595 1811 1009 1652 55 163 721 1039 464 915 12 19 684 678 464 754 14 31 \n2 Arunachal Pradesh 86 110 46 62 2 2 91 79 39 64 0 0 128 94 43 55 0 0 \n3 Assam 4222 3531 1439 1862 48 61 3895 6587 1351 1458 93 100 5039 5624 1952 2117 135 158 \n4 Bihar 4419 4925 2518 4560 85 146 4735 5381 2272 3467 81 143 5158 4777 2551 3279 75 107 \n5 Chhattisgarh 1881 498 449 484 52 78 1393 886 766 950 85 103 1354 762 608 800 151 171 \n6 Goa 70 29 12 20 2 4 86 34 16 19 0 0 70 27 27 32 1 1 \n7 Gujarat 2230 2646 1499 2508 46 70 2187 2370 1569 2406 35 49 1569 1552 1081 1582 18 25 \n8 Haryana 1957 1067 755 1066 116 183 1922 969 634 868 148 186 2336 694 511 667 120 153 \n9 Himachal Pradesh 290 131 82 139 10 23 227 95 55 86 3 5 239 84 71 88 3 3 \n10 Jammu & Kashmir 949 686 358 680 14 17 813 707 321 606 5 6 1071 798 431 725 8 9 \n11 Jharkhand 926 1111 564 905 116 149 885 797 561 653 118 166 930 747 590 669 73 90 \n12 Karnataka 1359 902 471 830 13 27 1457 1099 491 801 7 9 1611 785 476 655 10 11 \n13 Kerala 185 243 151 221 4 4 143 159 135 164 0 0 192 200 163 180 17 5 \n14 Madhya Pradesh 2873 1860 1218 1876 197 323 5688 3530 2577 3439 268 442 4547 3036 2166 3203 272 436 \n15