\nUttar Pradesh\n \n13.20\n \n0.22\n \n0.00\n \n13.42\n \n28\n.\n \nWest Bengal\n \n0.00\n \n4.08\n \n4.18\n \n8.26\n \n29\n.\n \nOthers\n \n0.00\n \n1.76\n \n0.00\n \n1.76\n \nTotal \n \n37.08\n \n27.29\n \n179.26\n \n \n243.63\n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|11|UNSTARRED |86", "t": "Closed Textile Mills", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 86  \n(TO BE ANSWERED ON 08.2.2017) \n  \n \nREFORMS IN PUBLIC BROADCASTING  \n \n*86. SHRI CH. MALLA REDDY: \n      SHRI MUTHAMSETTI SRINIVASA RAO (AVANTHI): \n \nWill the MINISTER OF INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether several committees, including Sam Pitroda Panel have recommended structural \nreforms in the Public Broadcasting and if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(b) whether a panel of Secretaries has also suggested the Government to make public \nbroadcasters, Doordarshan (DD) and All India Radio (AIR), corporate entities so as to \nreduce their dependence on Government funds and give them elbow room to take \ndecisions; and  \n \n(c)   if so, the reaction of the Government thereto? \n \n \nANSWER  \n \nTHE MINISTER OF INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING                                      \n[SHRI M. VENKAIAH NAIDU] \n \n(a) to (c): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n  \n  \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO  IN REPLY TO PARTS ( a) TO ( c) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 86 FOR ANSWER ON 08.2.2017. \n(a): In the recent past, the Government had set up an Expert Committee under the \nChairmanship of Shri Sam Pitroda on 28.01.2013, to review the functioning of Prasar Bharati. \nThe Committee had submitted its Report on 24.1.2014 and recommended framing a long-term \nvision and strategy for Prasar Bharati that would include and be dependent upon the \ntechnology choices, ass essment of human resources, review of programming, including \nreview of existing channels and also a study of independent sources of finances for Prasar \nBharati. Details of recommendations of the Expert Committee may be seen in its Report \nwhich is available  on the website of the Ministry of Information and Broadcasting at the \nfollowing address: www.mib.nic.in>>Documents>>Broadcasting-Documents>>Report of \nExpert Committee on Prasar Bharati Vol 1 and Report of Expert Committee on Prasar \nBharati Vol 2. \n(b):  A Group of Secretaries has been considering, apart from issues relating to other \nMinistries, the issues concerning the Ministry of Information and Broadcasting also but no \nReport of the Group has been received by this Ministry. \n(c):   In view of (b) above, the question does not arise. \n \n***** \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|11|UNSTARRED |860", "t": "Decline in the Production of Urea", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CHEMICALS & FERTILIZERS \nDEPARTMENT OF FERTILIZERS \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.    860 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON : 07.02.2017 \n \nDecline in the Production of Urea \n \n860   SHRI VISHNU DAYAL RAM: \n           \n \nWill the Minister of CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS be pleased to state : \n \n(a)   whether there has been a sharp decline in the production of Urea and other  fertilizers \nin the private sector companies during the last three years as  compared to the targets fixed;  \n \n(b)   if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor; and  \n \n(c)   the details of the subsidy given to these companies during the said period ? \n \n \nANSWER \n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF CHEMICALS & FERTILIZERS, SHIPPING, \nROAD TRANSPORT & HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI MANSUKH L. MANDAVIYA) \n \n(a) & (b):  The details of target and actual production of Urea & P&K fertilizers in the Private \nSector companies  during last three years are as under:-  \n                                                        \n (in ‘LMT’) \n \nYear Urea P&K \nTarget Production Target Production \n2013-14 100.52 93.52 84.64 64.17 \n2014-15 105.37 93.05 91.00 69.26 \n2015-16 103.80 104.60 90.55 72.55 \n \nFrom the above table, it is seen that there was some shortfall in the  production of Urea  during \n2013-14  and 2014-15 in the private sector due to non-availability of adequate Natural Gas. Production of \nUrea exceeded the target in 2015-16 following the announcement of New Urea Policy- 2015 with the \nobjectives of maximizing indigenous urea production; promoting energy efficiency in the urea units; and to \nrationalize the subsidy burden on the Government. \n \n\n \n-: 2 :-  \n \nProduction of P&K fertilizers is less than target due to huge quantities of unsold stocks of P&K \nfertilizers in the field, temporary shutdown of some plants for maintenance and limitations on account of \navailability of raw materials. This is a temporary  phenomenon.   \n \n The gap between demand (requirement) and production is met through import. \n \n(c):  Total  subsidy granted to indigenous Urea producing units and total subsidy given for  \nindigenous P&K Fertilizers units during the last three years are as under:- \n \n                                                                                               ( Rupees in Crore) \n \nYear 2013-14 2014-15 2015-16 \nTotal subsidy paid to indigenous urea \nproducing units \n26500.00 38200.01 38200.01 \nTotal subsidy paid to indigenous P&K \nFertilizers \n15500.00 12000.00 11969.01 \n \n \n******* \n \n \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|11|UNSTARRED |861", "t": "Modernisation of Police Force", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. †861 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 7TH FEBRUARY, 2017/ MAGHA 18, 1938 (SAKA) \n \nMODERNISATION OF POLICE FORCE \n \n†861.  DR. BHOLA SINGH: \nSHRIMATI V. SATHYA BAMA: \nSHRI P.R. SUNDARAM: \nSHRI P.K. BIJU: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) the total funds allocated and utilised  under the S cheme of \nModernisation of  State Police Force during each of the last  three years \nand the current year, State -wise including Karnataka, Andhra Pradesh,  \nTelangana and Bihar; \n \n(b) the details of the projects/  components undertaken in various States  \nfor Modernisation of State Police Force and infrastructure development in \nthe country, State-wise including Kerala; \n \n(c) whether the G overnment has  received any request from the States  \nparticularly Tamil Nadu for the  Modernisation of State Police Force and  if \nso, the details thereof and the action taken thereon, State-wise; \n \n(d) whether the Government has also  received request from the State \nGovernment of Tamil Nadu to allot Rs. 10,000 crores in the next few years \nfor modernisation of its police force and if so,  the details and present \nstatus thereof; \n \n(e) whether it is a fact that the Government has approved an allocation of \nRs. 12,379.30 crores for the Modernisation  of State Police Forces for the \nperiod from 2012-2013 to 2016-2017 which is woefully  inadequate for the \nneeds of the entire Police Force, if so, the details thereof and the reaction \nof the Government in this regard; and \n \n \n..2 \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n-2- \nL.S.US.Q.NO.861 FOR 07.02.2017 \n \n (f) the other measures taken by the  Government including technical \nassistance for Modernisation of Police Forces in the country? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI HANSRAJ GANGARAM AHIR) \n \n(a), (b) & (c): ‘Police’ and ‘Public Order’ are state subjects. However, \nGovernment of India supplements the efforts of State Governments by \nproviding central assistance under the Modernisation of Police \nForces(MPF) scheme to strengthen police infrastructure. A statement \nshowing t he total funds earmarked/allocated and disbursed to various \nStates during each of the last thr ee years and the current year, state-\nwise is at Annexure I.  A statement showing allocation and release of \nfunds for Megacity Policing, which is a component under MPF Scheme, is \nat Annexure-II.  The major items covered under the Scheme are mobility, \nweapons, equipments, training infrastructure, computerization and \nforensic science and infrastructure development and each State \nGovernment finalize their annual plans/projects as per their requirements \nand strategic priorities. For the current financial year, all the states \nsubmitted their State Action Plans and funds are being released based on \nallocation of each state and subject to  submission of Utilization \nCertificates (UCs) in respect of  funds  released  to each state prior to the  \n...3/ \n \n \n\n \n-3- \nL.S.US.Q.NO 861 FOR 07.02.2017 \n \nlast financial year. A s regards State Government of Tamil Nadu, during \nyear 2016 -17, a total amount of Rs. 68.54 crore has been released, \nincluding Rs. 41.80 crore for megacity policing component for Chennai.   \n \n(d)& (e): The central Government has received request from the Sta te \nGovernment of Tamil Nadu to allot Rs. 10,000 crore. Further, Rs. \n12,379.30 crore was approved by the Cabinet Committee on Security \n(CCS) under the Scheme for all States for 5 year period i.e. 2012 -17. \nFunds allocated by Ministry of Finance under MPF Sch eme are distributed \nas per CCS approved pre-decided formula (Pro-rata basis). For the current \nFinancial Year, the State Government of Tamil Nadu has allocation of Rs. \n27.96 for equipment, out of which, Rs. 26.74 crore has been released.  In \naddition to this, funds to the tune of Rs. 4.35 crore has been allocated for \nweaponry to be supplied through Ordnance Factory Board. Since no \nadditional funds are provided for the Scheme, it is not possible to allocate \nadditional funds requested by the State Govt. of Tamil Nadu.  \n \n(f)    Other measures taken by the Central Government to modernize state \npolice forces include Crime and Criminal Tracking Network and \nSystems(CCTNS), operating a nationwide Satellite network for police \ncommunication, implementation of National Emergency Response \nSystem, etc.  \n****** \n\nANNEXURE-I \nL.S.US.Q.NO 861 FOR 07.02.2017 \nFunds released to various State Govts. and utilization thereof under MPF Scheme during last three years and the current year  \n2016-17.(Rs. in crore) \nState Name 2013-14 2014-15 2015-16 2016-17 \nAllocatio\nn \nReleased Allocati\non \nReleased Allocati\non \nReleased \nAllocation Normal Conting\nency \nNormal Conting\nency \nNormal Contingency \nAndhra \nPradesh 120.40 \n77.92 8.00 \n102.81 \n54.17 0.00 22.68 31.26 1.30 22.68 \nArunachal \nPradesh 11.26 \n7.95 2.82 \n9.62 \n6.58 3.11 3.64 3.05 0.00 3.64 \nAssam  75.76 51.91 8.02 64.70 43.29 "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|11|UNSTARRED |862", "t": "Wildlife Regions", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \n          LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.862 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON  07.02.2017 \n \nWildlife Regions \n \n862. SHRI HARI OM PANDAY: \n DR. RATNA DE (NAG): \n \n           \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased \nto state: \n \n(a) whether the Ministry has any Centralis ed Mechanism for the booking of Forest \nguest houses present across the wild life regions of the country; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether such mechanism w ill be beneficial to promote forest tourism in the \ncountry and if so, the details thereof?  \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR ENVIRONMENT, FOREST \nAND CLIMATE CHANGE  \n(SHRI ANIL MADHAV DAVE) \n \n(a) & (b) No Sir. Management of Protected Areas is the responsibility of the concerned \nState Government. Every State has its own mechanism and in most Tiger \nReserves, online booking facility is available. \n \n(c) The Government of India is not involved in revenue coll ection vis-a-vis entry \nand stay in National Parks and Wildlife Sanctuaries including Tiger Reserves \nand it is the subject matter falling under the State Government. Guidelines for \ntourism in Tiger Reserves have been issued and is available in public domain  \nat www.projecttiger.nic.in. \n \n**** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|11|UNSTARRED |863", "t": "Ceasefire Violation", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. †863 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 7TH  FEBRUARY, 2017/ MAGHA 18, 1938 (SAKA) \n \nCEASEFIRE VIOLATION \n \n†863.  SHRI RAM KUMAR SHARMA: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether there has been an increase  in incidents of ceasefire \nviolations particularly on Indo-Pak border; \n \n(b) if so, the total number of such incidents that took place during the last \nthree years and the current year; and \n \n(c) the number of security personnels  and civilians killed due to these \nincidents during the said period? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI HANSRAJ GANGARAM AHIR) \n \n(a) to (c): The details of ceasefire violations reported from across the \nborders and the number of security personnel killed in Jammu and \nKashmir(J&K), during the last three years and the current year is as \nunder:- \n \nNo. of Ceasefire violations \nYear Along the Line Control in the State of J&K \nunder operational control of Army \nAlong the International Border in the State of \nJ&K under operational control of BSF \n2014 153 430 \n2015 152 253 \n2016  228 221 \n2017(till \nJanuary) \n08  02 \n \nCasualties of Civilians and Security Personnel due to Ceasefire Violations \n \nYear Civilians Army Personnel BSF Personnel \n2014 14 1 2 \n2015 16 6 4 \n2016  13 8 5 \n2017(till January) 0 0 0 \n \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|11|UNSTARRED |864", "t": "Agriculture Cropping Pattern", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n864\n                      \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE  \n7\nTH\n \nFEBRUARY\n, 2017\n \n \nAGRICULTURE CROPPING \nPATTERN\n \n \n864. \n \nSHRI KAPIL MORESHWAR\n \nPATIL:\n \n           \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n  \n‡ãðŠãäÓã \nएवं \n\u0000कसान \nक\u0000याण \n½ãâ¨ããè\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n \n(a) \n \nthe total cultivable land in the\n \ncountry along with the extent of land\n \nactively \ncultivated during the last three\n \nyears, State\n-\nwise;\n \n \n(b) \n \nwhether the Government proposes\n \nto undertake any programme to educate\n \nand \ninform farmers about best practices in\n \nagriculture cropping patterns in order to\n \nensure \noptimum production and maintain\n \nfert\nility;\n \n \n(c) \n \nwhether the Government also\n \nproposes to encourage crop rotation to\n \ncheck \ncycles of high and low productions;\n \nand\n \n \n(d) \n \nif so, the details thereof and the action\n \ntaken thereon?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF  STATE IN THE  MINISTRY OF  AGRICULTURE AND  F\nARMERS  WELFARE\n \n \n‡ãðŠãäÓã  \nएवं \n\u0000कसान \nक\u0000याण \n½ãâ¨ããÊã¾ã ½ãñâ Àã•¾ã ½ãâ¨ããè          \n \n( SHRI\n \nS.S. \nAHLUWALIA\n)  \n \n \n(a):\n \nAs per report on Land Use Statistics for 2013\n-\n14 (latest available), the State\n-\nwise \ndetails of cultivable land/agricultural land and \ncultivated land in the country for the last \nthree years i.e. from 2011\n-\n12 to 2013\n-\n14 are given in the Annexure.\n \n \n(b):\n \nIn order to increase production and productivity of crops the Government is \nimplementing various programmes to educate and support farmers\n \nin the country to \nadopt modern technologies and practices. Through National Mission on Agricultural \nExtension and Technology comprising of activities such as farmers training, \ndemonstrations, exposure visits, Kisan melas, etc, latest agriculture technolog\nies are \ndisseminated to farmers. Under the schemes like National Food Security Mission and \nBringing Green Revolution to Eastern India, trainings are imparted to farmers on crop \nmanagement practices and also to create awareness about the new high yielding \nv\narieties/hybrids and new practices. The Indian Council of Agricultural Research (ICAR) \nalso demonstrates new technologies through established network of Krishi Vigyan \nKendras (KVKs) to benefit farming community.\n \n  Contd…2/ -  \n\n \n \n-\n2\n-\n \n \n(c) \n&\n \n(d): \n \nIn order to en\nsure optimum crop production and maintain soil health & \nfertility, the Indian Council of Agricultural Research (ICAR)\n  \nthrough its research \ninstitutions and All India Coordinated Research Projects/Network projects/Consortia \nResearch Platforms and State Agr\nicultural Universities is developing cost effective, \nscientific farming practices for different crops/cropping systems in farmers’ participatory \nmode keeping in view the farmers’ resource availability, traditional indigenous \ntechnology knowhow and grassroo\nt farm innovations. Benefits of successful research \nare popularized through KVKs, State \nextension\n \nagencies etc. Besides, ICAR also \nimparts training and \norganizes\n \nFront Line Demonstrations (FLDs) to educate farmers on \nall these aspects.\n \nIn addition to above\n, the ICAR is also promoting adoption of crop rotation for \nsustainable higher crop production in the country. Crop rotation is also one of the \nprinciples of Conservation Agriculture. The council is addressing this issue under an on\n-\ngoing Consortia Research\n \nPlatforms on Conservation Agriculture.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n  \n \n  \n\n \n \nAnnexure\n \n \nState\n-\nwise details of Cultivable/Agricultural Land and Cultivated land in the country \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n(Thousand Hectares)\n \nSTATES/Uts\n \nCultivable/ Agricultural \nLand\n \nCultivated Land\n \n2011\n-\n12\n \n2012\n-\n13\n \n2013\n-\n14\n \n2011\n-\n12\n \n2012\n-\n13\n \n2013\n-\n14\n \nANDHRA PRADESH\n \n15894